---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/quiet-hours";
const title = "Quiet hours: operate the recipient\u2019s dispatch window";
const desc = "Control when a message can leave the queue, what happens while it waits and whether it is still useful when the window opens.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <h1>Quiet hours: operate the recipient’s dispatch window</h1>
    <p>Control when a message can leave the queue, what happens while it waits and whether it is still useful when the window opens.</p>
    <h2>Use a policy for each destination and message category</h2>
    <p>There is no universal quiet-hours window that clears every promotional text. Maintain a reviewed policy by destination, message category, day and applicable provider or carrier restriction. Keep the policy owner, effective date and evidence with the configuration. Provider settings help enforce a policy; their defaults do not establish legal clearance for your audience.</p>
    <p>Check the destination and the recipient’s local time rather than the store clock or venue time. A campaign for an event in another city still reaches people in their own time zones. Legal interpretation belongs with a qualified reviewer; this guide describes the dispatch controls to implement once the policy is agreed.</p>
    <h2>Make the time-zone input auditable</h2>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Operating decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Known recipient zone</td><td>Store the source and resolve the date with daylight-saving rules</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inferred location</td><td>Record the inference and its uncertainty; phone geography need not be current location</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Missing or conflicting location</td><td>Use the reviewed conservative fallback or hold for resolution</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Changed profile</td><td>Decide which value applies at dispatch and log the decision</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>Use named time zones where the sending system supports them rather than keeping a permanent numeric offset. Inspect the provider’s fallback behavior instead of assuming it matches yours. If no safe window can be established, hold the message; an unknown zone should not silently become headquarters time.</p>
    <h2>Campaigns and flows can behave differently</h2>
    <p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/55578813576091">Klaviyo’s campaign documentation</a> describes checking quiet hours at each recipient’s send time. Its <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408737146651">flow documentation</a> describes separate flow behavior and regional exceptions. Review the specific message type rather than assuming one account setting governs every path.</p>
    <p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/7731269-manage-sms-sending-limits-frequency-quiet-hours">Omnisend documents postponement to an available local delivery time</a>, with a distinct daily frequency limit and destination restrictions. A recipient hitting the frequency limit is not necessarily just waiting for quiet hours to end. Record skipped, postponed and failed outcomes separately.</p>
    <h2>Give queued messages an expiry</h2>
    <p>Write the latest useful send time in the campaign brief. If an offer ends before the next permitted window, cancel that recipient’s offer message or prepare a different approved message. Do not send stale urgency after the sale closes. For stock alerts, check that the requested variant remains available when releasing the queue.</p>
    <p>A purchase, withdrawal of permission, support exclusion or another campaign can change eligibility while a message waits. Recheck the relevant exits at dispatch and keep an audit trail. One service delaying a text does not reserve the contact’s next slot in another service; the calendar owner must reconcile those queues.</p>
    <h2>Proposed boundary rehearsal</h2>
    <ol><li>Use controlled profiles in several supported destination zones, plus missing and conflicting-zone profiles.</li><li>Schedule just before and just after a configured boundary. Inspect the provider decision and the planned local and UTC timestamps.</li><li>Repeat across daylight-saving transitions and a restricted day where relevant. Include ambiguous or nonexistent local times.</li><li>Queue a short-lived offer, then let it expire before release. Verify cancellation rather than a stale send.</li><li>Purchase and opt out while a reminder is delayed. Confirm the pending action responds to both events.</li><li>Exercise a frequency-limit collision and a provider rejection separately from quiet-hours postponement.</li></ol>
    <p>These are proposed checks, not delivery results. Where a provider cannot safely simulate a boundary, inspect its documented decision and use a controlled permitted test with support. Do not disable live protections to force the scenario.</p>
    <h2>Monitor the backlog after the window opens</h2>
    <p>Log campaign and message identifiers, policy version, zone source, eligibility check, planned dispatch, expiry and actual status. A growing deferred queue may be a timing-policy issue; a growing rejected queue may be a destination or account issue. Avoid retrying everything as though it were a temporary delay.</p>
    <p>Assign an operator to stop the release when an offer expires, inventory changes or suppression sync fails. Releasing a large postponed audience at once can collide with a fresh campaign, so inspect combined contact pressure before resuming. Frequency limits and quiet hours solve different problems.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint guide</a> owns contact pressure; <a href="/guides/consent">consent operations</a> owns permission evidence. This page owns local dispatch and queue validity. For the concrete vendor comparison, use <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> and demonstrate the exact configured path before choosing.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
